.TH MACHO\-MCP 1 "2026-10-03" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho\-mcp \- serve MachO\-Tools over the Model Context Protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho\-mcp
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B macho\-mcp
serves this package's eight readers to a coding agent over the Model Context
Protocol, on stdin and stdout. It is meant to be launched by an MCP client
rather than run by hand: the process reads JSON\-RPC messages, one per line,
and writes them back the same way.
.PP
The tools are
.BR macho\-describe ,
.BR macho\-sym ,
.BR macho\-symlookup ,
.BR macho\-findcall ,
.BR macho\-findliteral ,
.BR macho\-mapliteral ,
.B macho\-a2o
and
.BR macho\-o2a .
Each returns the same JSON envelope the command\-line tools emit under
.BR \-\-json ,
alongside a short text summary.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage on standard error and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version on standard error and exit.
.PP
Every other argument is a usage error. Standard output carries the protocol and
nothing else; anything written there that is not a valid message is diverted to
standard error and counted.
.SH PROTOCOL VERSIONS
Both protocol eras are served, because clients in the wild still use both. A
request carrying
.B _meta["io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion"]
is treated as the modern revision (2026\-07\-28, per\-request metadata, no
handshake); an
.B initialize
request selects the legacy handshake, back to 2024\-11\-05.
.B server/discover
answers for either. An unsupported version is rejected with \-32022 and a list
of the versions that are supported.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_BINARY
Default binary, so a client need not repeat the path on every call.
.TP
.B MACHO_APP
An application bundle, used when
.B MACHO_BINARY
is unset.
.SH EXAMPLES
Register with a client:
.PP
.nf
.RS
claude mcp add macho \-\- node /absolute/path/to/src/mcp.mjs
.RE
.fi
.PP
Or in a project's
.BR .mcp.json :
.PP
.nf
.RS
{ "mcpServers": { "macho": {
    "command": "node",
    "args": ["/absolute/path/to/src/mcp.mjs"],
    "env": { "MACHO_BINARY": "/path/to/a/binary" } } } }
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The client closed standard input.
.TP
.B 2
Invoked with no client attached, or with
.BR \-\-help / \-\-version .
.SH NOTES
A client that asks for a tool it does not recognise receives a protocol error
naming the tools that exist. A call that finds nothing is
.I not
an error: it returns
.B "ok: true"
with an empty result, because a caller that cannot tell "found nothing" from
"could not look" has no way to act on the difference. A genuine failure is
reported with
.B "ok: false"
and a reason code \- for example
.B io
when the path cannot be read, or
.B unknown\-encoding
when it can be read and is not a Mach\-O.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR macho\-describe (1),
.BR macho\-sym (1),
.BR macho\-symlookup (1),
.BR macho\-findcall (1),
.BR macho\-findliteral (1),
.BR macho\-mapliteral (1),
.BR macho\-a2o (1),
.BR macho\-o2a (1)
